#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# fixed_point_list

#% text
# `fixed_point_list(f, x)` gives `[x, f(x), f(f(x)), ...]`, stopping when a value repeats the one before it.

#% text
# `fixed_point_list(f, x, n)` stops after at most `n` applications.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Numbers that are equal, or balls that overlap, count as a repeat, so iterations on inexact numbers stop when they settle.

#% item
# The last element is the fixed point `fix_point(f, x)` reaches.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
fixed_point_list(x => (x + 2/x)/2, 1.0)
#% output
#= [1.0, 1.5, 1.4166666666666665, 1.4142156862745097, 1.4142135623746899, 1.414213562373095, 1.414213562373095]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# At most four steps:

#% cell
fixed_point_list(x => (x + 2/x)/2, 1, 4)
#% output
#= [1, 3/2, 17/12, 577/408, 665857/470832]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The last element is `fix_point`'s value:

#% cell
last(fixed_point_list(cos, 1.0)) == fix_point(cos, 1.0)
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `fix_point`, `nest_while_list`, `nest_list`
